Identify who Dürrüaden Kadın is and determine the historical or biographical context of her husband's death.
Dürrüaden Kadın was the third wife of Sultan Mehmed V of the Ottoman Empire. She was born as Hatice Voçibe on 16 May 1860 in Sochi, Ubykhia, Circassia, and was a member of the Abkhazian Voçibe noble family. She was brought to Istanbul as a child and entrusted to the imperial harem by her father, Mustafa Bey Voçibe. Dürrüaden married Sultan Mehmed V on 10 October 1876, when she was sixteen years old. She gave birth to their only son, Şehzade Mahmud Necmeddin, on 23 June 1878. After Mehmed's accession to the throne in 1909, she was given the title of "Üçüncü Kadın" (Third Consort). Dürrüaden Kadın died on 17 October 1909 in the Vadildebağı Palace and was buried in the mausoleum of Gülüstü Hanım, Fatih Mosque, Istanbul.

Regarding the historical or biographical context of her husband's death: Sultan Mehmed V died on 3 March 1918, during World War I, while he was in exile in the Caucasus region. He had been living in the area since the Ottoman Empire's defeat in the war, and his death occurred as a result of illness.
