Prince Nikolaus Wilhelm of Nassau, born on 20 September 1832 and died on 17 September 1905, was the only son of William, Duke of Nassau, by his second wife, Princess Pauline of Württemberg. His lineage traces back to the House of Nassau, a prominent European royal family. He married morganatically in London on 1 July 1868 Natalya Alexandrovna Pushkina, who was the daughter of the renowned poet Alexander Pushkin and Natalya Nikolaevna Goncharova. Through this marriage, he became the progenitor of the Counts von Merenberg, a title created by George Victor, Prince of Waldeck and Pyrmont in 1868. The Merenberg line is historically significant as it connects to the Russian nobility and has ties to notable figures such as Abram Petrovich Gannibal and Petro Doroshenko, the Hetman of Ukrainian Cossacks. Prince Nikolaus's son, who married a daughter of Tsar Alexander II of Russia, sought recognition as heir presumptive to the Luxembourgeois throne, although this claim was ultimately dismissed by a 1907 law. Prince Nikolaus was also a great-grandson of Charles Christian, 3rd Prince of Nassau-Weilburg, who married Princess Carolina of Orange-Nassau in 1760.
