The Grammy Award for Best Rap/Sung Performance is a category that recognizes outstanding collaborative efforts between rap and sung artists who typically do not perform together. This award highlights the synergy and creativity involved in blending different musical styles, emphasizing the importance of collaboration in the music industry.

The award was introduced in the 52nd Grammy Awards in 2002, with the first winner being Americans Eve and Gwen Stefani for their song "Let Me Blow Ya Mind." The category requires that the collaborative artist(s) be recognized as featured artists, underscoring the significance of their contribution to the performance.

Jay-Z holds the record for the most Grammy Awards in this category, having received seven Grammys—four times as a lead artist and three times as a featured artist. His achievements demonstrate the impact of his work in merging rap and sung performances.

This award is significant because it celebrates innovation and artistic collaboration, reflecting the evolving nature of music and the value placed on cross-genre partnerships in the Grammy Awards.
