The Black Tavern Society has been actively involved in the preservation of the Black Tavern, which is an historic tavern located at 138-142 Dudley Center Road in Dudley, Massachusetts. The main block of the tavern was built around 1803 and is recognized as one of the town's finest examples of Federal period architecture. Initially, the tavern served as a major stop on the stagecoach route between Boston and Hartford, Connecticut.

In 1985, the Black Tavern was listed on the National Register of Historic Places, acknowledging its historical significance. This listing was expanded in 2010 to include the adjacent barn and annex, which the Black Tavern Society acquired in 2000. This expansion reflects the society's efforts to preserve not only the main structure but also the surrounding properties that contribute to the historical context of the site. The society's acquisition and maintenance of these areas demonstrate their commitment to preserving the heritage and architectural integrity of the Black Tavern.
